    "finalAssistantVisibleText": "**Background on \"The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle\" by Stuart Turton**\n\n- **Publication History**: \n  - \"The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle\" was published in 2018 by Sourcebooks Landmark. The book's ISBN is 978-1-4926-6854-6.\n\n- **Synopsis**: \n  - The novel is a mystery that features a unique narrative structure where the protagonist relives the same day multiple times—eight in total—each time inhabiting a different guest at the Blackheath estate. The objective is to solve the murder of Evelyn Hardcastle before the day resets. The story blends elements of classic Agatha Christie-style country house mystery with a time-loop structure, providing commentary on perspective, identity, and moral responsibility.\n\n- **Critical Reception**: \n  - Upon publication, the novel received considerable attention and praise from critics for its innovative recursive narrative and thematic depth. It has been described as a key text in contemporary fiction and has been widely reviewed across major literary outlets.\n\n- **Awards and Shortlistings**: \n  - \"The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle\" has garnered several accolades and was shortlisted for prestigious awards such as the Costa First Novel Award and the Specsavers National Book Award.\n\nFor more detailed information, you can explore the following resources:\n\n1. [Wikipedia entry](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Seven_Deaths_of_Evelyn_Hardcastle) - provides an overview of the book and its critical reception.\n2. [Critical Overview on Open Books](https://openbooks.net/reviews/seven-deaths-of-evelyn-hardcastle) - offers a detailed analysis of the book's themes and narrative structure.\n3. [Publisher's Page](https://openbooks.net/books/seven-deaths-of-evelyn-hardcastle) - contains official descriptions and availability of different formats.\n\nThis information should assist in contextualizing the novel for your seminar on experimental narrative structures in contemporary fiction.",
